    "confidence": 92,
    "conclusion": "Site A’s speed advantage is robust across order size, product mix, shift, staffing pressure, exclusions, and matched-worker analyses, generally remaining about 5–9%. The scanner causally improves individual picking speed by roughly 4% (95% CI: 1–7%), with robustness estimates near 3–5%, so it is a meaningful contributor. However, it is not the primary or sole explanation: a residual Site A advantage of about 3–6% persists after adjustment, and Site A remained approximately 5% faster during scanner outages. Reject the scanner-only explanation. Retain scanner use as a partial driver and retain other site-level mechanisms—especially those associated with complex orders—as plausible competing explanations; the available evidence does not identify which of those mechanisms accounts for the residual advantage."
